.SH NAME
\fBxnvme-perf-storage \fP- Run I/O benchmarks against NVMe devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.fam C
\fBxnvme-perf-storage\fP <command> [<args>]
.fam T
.fi
.fam T
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run I/O benchmarks against NVMe devices
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B
\fBxnvme-perf-storage-run\fP(1)
Run a time-bounded async IO benchmark against one or more NVMe devices. Devices are distributed across CPU threads pinned by \fB--cpumask\fP or \fB--cpulist\fP. With \fB--be\fP 'io_uring_dmabuf' the IO buffers are allocated on the GPU given by \fB--gpu-id\fP and exported as a dma-buf for the kernel to DMA into.
.TP
.B
\fBxnvme-perf-storage-verify\fP(1)
For each device: writes \fB--count\fP sequential IOs with a per-LBA pattern, then reads them back and compares against the expected data.
.TP
.B
\fBxnvme-perf-storage-cuda-run\fP(1)
Run a time-bounded GPU NVMe I/O benchmark against one or more devices. All devices run in parallel: one CUDA block per queue, all queues launched in a single kernel. All devices must use the same LBA size.
.TP
.B
\fBxnvme-perf-storage-cuda-verify\fP(1)
Write an LBA-stamped pattern to each device through GPU queues and read it back, verifying that the data matches. Uses the same queue topology as cuda-run so results are directly comparable.
.RE
.PP

